The issue proposed a reading-speed budget (about 17 characters/s) with shorten() beyond it. It was left out: on the test video, faithful translations without any budget read at 11-19 characters/s in en, fr and hi, with one French line at 22.7, so a budget isn't needed yet (deferred in ROADMAP.md). The dubbing budget was the cause of the lost content: shortening to a speaking slot dropped phrases like 'is coming to you from Sendai'. The prompt passes each line's duration and the text of neighbouring lines, so the model can keep lines short and follow sentences split across segments, without merging or moving content between lines, since each translation keeps its source segment's timing. Glossary names stay in their glossary spelling, so Hindi shows Latin names; writing them in the target script was left for a separate issue.
